of the market.

215
00:19:37,000 --> 00:19:39,000
Because that's essentially the position
you've got on.

216
00:19:44,000 --> 00:19:45,000
What about hedging out the market risk?

217
00:19:45,000 --> 00:19:45,000
What about hedging out the market risk?

218
00:19:53,000 --> 00:20:01,000
Let's say you've got a £20,000 limit on a
spread trade.

219
00:20:02,000 --> 00:20:06,000
Or a £10,000 limit on a single position.

220
00:20:07,000 --> 00:20:10,000
We'll go on to position limits and theme
limits next.

221
00:20:11,000 --> 00:20:15,000
How do you hedge out the market risk on
two stocks?

222
00:20:16,000 --> 00:20:26,000
So beta is just for every unit that the
market moves, the historical move of the
stock.

223
00:20:27,000 --> 00:20:37,000
So if the market moves one, if a stock has
a beta of 1.15, history says the stock
will move 1.15.

224
00:20:38,000 --> 00:20:42,000
But when you're hedging out market risk,
you've got beta values of two stocks.

225
00:20:43,000 --> 00:20:44,000
So how do you hedge out the market risk?

226
00:20:46,000 --> 00:20:53,000
But what you do is you take the beta ratio
of the long and divide it by the beta
ratio of the short.

227
00:20:56,000 --> 00:20:58,000
And you get a beta of the spread.

228
00:20:59,000 --> 00:21:01,000
You get a beta of the ratio.

229
00:21:01,000 --> 00:21:21,000
So in terms of beta hedging, what we're
going to send you is the beta hedge
example that you've just seen.

230
00:21:22,000 --> 00:21:25,000
A beta hedge step-by-step guide.

231
00:21:26,000 --> 00:21:32,000
And that guide is going to show you how to
actually calculate your own beta in an
Excel spreadsheet.

232
00:21:33,000 --> 00:21:36,000
By taking the historic data of the market
and the stock.

233
00:21:37,000 --> 00:21:39,000
So you can go out publicly and get the
betas.

234
00:21:40,000 --> 00:21:44,000
You can get three months, six months,
twelve months, three year, five year.

235
00:21:47,000 --> 00:21:50,000
But you can calculate your own if you
want, if you struggle to find the beta.

236
00:21:52,000 --> 00:21:54,000
Now, caution, beta isn't perfect.

237
00:21:55,000 --> 00:22:02,000
So it doesn't mean that if we're trying to
hedge out the market risk, we can be
successful all the time.

238
00:22:08,000 --> 00:22:13,000
Because the beta value is just the
historical data of the stock in relation
to the market.

239
00:22:16,000 --> 00:22:17,000
If it does change.

240
00:22:19,000 --> 00:22:21,000
If it does change, you'll be told in your
portfolio that it's changed.

241
00:22:22,000 --> 00:22:23,000
Because you'll be losing money because of
it.

242
00:22:24,000 --> 00:22:32,000
And you won't understand why, when the
market moves one unit, the historical beta
doesn't work quite the way you expected.

243
00:22:33,000 --> 00:22:37,000
So it's not exact science.

244
00:22:38,000 --> 00:22:39,000
Because beta can change.

245
00:22:39,000 --> 00:22:39,000
Because beta can change.

246
00:22:39,000 --> 00:22:51,000
So we rely on it as a tool to attempt to
hedge out market risk as best we can.

247
00:22:52,000 --> 00:22:55,000
But we do not rely on it with our lives.

248
00:22:58,000 --> 00:23:05,000
And everyone forgets this in the
professional trading world, when they
spend years building sophisticated models,
and then beta just changes.

249
00:23:07,000 --> 00:23:14,000
But that's why you have other risk
parameters and risk management parameters
in your portfolio.

250
00:23:15,000 --> 00:23:16,000
To stop you being human.

251
00:23:16,000 --> 00:23:24,000
Not admitting that your beta calculations
are wrong is pulling from box A when
you're wrong.

252
00:23:25,000 --> 00:23:26,000
Does that make sense?

253
00:23:26,000 --> 00:23:43,000
So you'll get theme limits, single asset
position limits.

254
00:23:44,000 --> 00:23:51,000
What about a $100,000 exposure portfolio
with a 10% single position limit?

255
00:23:52,000 --> 00:24:09,000
So you just say to yourself, if my gross
exposure limit is $100,000, I'm never
going to go into one position over
$10,000.

256
00:24:10,000 --> 00:24:12,000
And as you make money, guess what?

257
00:24:12,000 --> 00:24:15,000
The notional value of your single position
limits go up.

258
00:24:18,000 --> 00:24:21,000
So you actually get rewarded for making
money by doing nothing.

259
00:24:22,000 --> 00:24:23,000
Which is great.

260
00:24:24,000 --> 00:24:25,000
That's what we want.

261
00:24:28,000 --> 00:24:39,000
So very typically, that's quite this
example that we've gone through today of a
$25,000 situation where you put it in a
margin account and in step one, you have
$100,000 exposure.

262
00:24:40,000 --> 00:24:43,000
It's a very typical profile of an
institute trader.

263
00:24:44,000 --> 00:24:48,000
So the average institute trader is about
30,000 pounds in their trading account.

264
00:24:49,000 --> 00:24:51,000
Some have 10, some have over 100.

265
00:24:52,000 --> 00:24:54,000
One guy has over 300,000 in his account.

266
00:24:55,000 --> 00:25:00,000
But the average profile is about 30,000
pounds when you strip that guy out.

267
00:25:01,000 --> 00:25:09,000
So typically, most people have between 100
and 150,000 pounds exposure, something
like that.

268
00:25:10,000 --> 00:25:13,000
And that's what your portfolio would look
like in a spreadsheet or in a trading
account.

269
00:25:14,000 --> 00:25:19,000
You just have five longs, five shorts, 10%
position limits.

270
00:25:20,000 --> 00:25:25,000
And if you're doing beta adjusted trades
on spreads, then your spread is 20,000
limit.

271
00:25:26,000 --> 00:25:30,000
And your cash for cash situation has to
come below 20,000 when you add them up.

272
00:25:31,000 --> 00:25:35,000
So your gross exposure on the spread is no
higher than your theme limit.

273
00:25:36,000 --> 00:25:41,000
What about a portfolio stop loss?

274
00:25:42,000 --> 00:25:55,000
So you would hope that in a well
diversified long short portfolio, that if
you had five longs and five shorts, that
some would be working, some wouldn't be
working, but net net, you might be making
money or losing a little bit.

275
00:25:56,000 --> 00:26:00,000
You put a stop loss on your portfolio.

276
00:26:01,000 --> 00:26:05,000
Large cap stocks, S&P 500, FTSE 100.

277
00:26:06,000 --> 00:26:15,000
If you're putting stop losses on each
position of 10%, what do you think would
be a sensible portfolio stop loss?

278
00:26:20,000 --> 00:26:23,000
Well, one position, if you get stopped
out, will cost your portfolio 1%.

279
00:26:24,000 --> 00:26:40,000
Stop losses we're trying, even when we put
one on the portfolio, to put a stop loss
on the portfolio that gives us a good
chance of making money, but not in the
realms of stupidity where we're not having
discipline.

280
00:26:41,000 --> 00:26:44,000
So a sensible portfolio stop loss might be
10%.

281
00:26:47,000 --> 00:26:49,000
Or somewhere between five and 10%.

282
00:26:50,000 --> 00:26:58,000
Because 10% just means you've got all of
your positions wrong and you've been
stopped out.

283
00:26:59,000 --> 00:27:00,000
So you're going to get stopped out anyway.

284
00:27:04,000 --> 00:27:08,000
Because they've all cost your portfolio
1%, all at the same time.

285
00:27:11,000 --> 00:27:17,000
Whatever you do, stop yourself out, take a
break for one or two months and reassess.

286
00:27:19,000 --> 00:27:20,000
Because you've obviously had a nightmare.

287
00:27:22,000 --> 00:27:28,000
If you threw 10 darts at a board and
selected five longs and five shorts with
darts, you'd get one right.

288
00:27:29,000 --> 00:27:42,000
So for you to be stopped out on 10
situations in a 10-position portfolio,
there must be something really, really
wrong with your selecting, with your
ideas.

289
00:27:43,000 --> 00:27:53,000
So you're stopped out, you go on holiday,
you think about it, think about whether
trading is the right profession for you.

290
00:27:54,000 --> 00:28:06,000
But the key is, is if you started off with
25,000 and you had 100,000 exposure, you'd
be down to 15 in your trading account.

291
00:28:08,000 --> 00:28:09,000
You're not out the game.

292
00:28:10,000 --> 00:28:11,000
You've still got cash.

293
00:28:12,000 --> 00:28:16,000
You've still got the ability, you've still
got chips to come back to the table.

294
00:28:17,000 --> 00:28:22,000
That's why you have a stop loss on your
portfolio.

295
00:28:23,000 --> 00:28:40,000
So if you've got 10% stop losses and the
profile of your portfolio is 10 positions,
five long, five short, over the S&P 500,
large cap stocks, or very large liquid
currencies and commodities.

296
00:28:41,000 --> 00:28:44,000
And you've got a 10% stop loss on those
positions.

297
00:28:45,000 --> 00:28:48,000
For all of them to go wrong, there must be
something really, really, really wrong.

298
00:28:52,000 --> 00:28:54,000
So stick a 10% stop loss on the entire
portfolio.

299
00:28:55,000 --> 00:28:58,000
Drop 10 grand, what's the worst thing
that's going to happen?

300
00:29:00,000 --> 00:29:01,000
You've still got 15.

301
00:29:06,000 --> 00:29:10,000
But you've pulled from box B because
trading might not be the right profession
for you.

302
00:29:14,000 --> 00:29:31,000
So for the risk management section, very
simply, all of those risk management
parameters basically dictate how your
portfolio will be structured at maximum
parameters.

303
00:29:32,000 --> 00:29:34,000
Why do we have those maximum parameters?

304
00:29:35,000 --> 00:29:37,000
Because we're trying to stop ourselves
from being human.

305
00:29:42,000 --> 00:29:49,000
So position limits, theme limits, net
exposure limits, gross exposure limits,
stick within those parameters.

306
00:29:50,000 --> 00:29:51,000
You'll be the opposite of human.

307
00:29:52,000 --> 00:30:06,000
Okay, welcome back to the computer screen.

308
00:30:07,000 --> 00:30:15,000
What we're going to have a look at now is
beta and specifically how we use beta to
hedge out market risk.

309
00:30:16,000 --> 00:30:21,000
So we're going to look at a simple example
first of one stock long versus one stock
short.

310
00:30:22,000 --> 00:30:40,000
And then later we're going to look at a
more complicated example where we're
representing our idea through a theme
where we've got two longs and two shorts
representing the same idea and how we
hedge out market risk in that scenario.

311
00:30:41,000 --> 00:30:43,000
So let's start with the simple example.

312
00:30:44,000 --> 00:30:55,000
And here we're looking at a trade where
we're thinking about going long Chevron
and short AstraZeneca.

313
00:30:56,000 --> 00:31:08,000
Now this trade, if you refer back to the
original notes, this trade is a cross
sector constituent spread trade.

314
00:31:09,000 --> 00:31:12,000
So slightly higher risk than other spread
trades.

315
00:31:13,000 --> 00:31:32,000
So if we're looking at going long Chevron
and short AstraZeneca, we're looking at
going long a cyclical stock versus short a
defensive stock because Chevron's in the
oil sector and AstraZeneca is in the
pharmaceutical sector.

316
00:31:33,000 --> 00:31:44,000
So the oil sector, Chevron's earnings, so
Chevron being in the oil sector, Chevron's
earnings are more sensitive to the
business cycle than AstraZeneca's.

317
00:31:45,000 --> 00:31:59,000
So if we're backing out what we're looking
at in terms of our views here, at this
stage we're clearly bullish on the world
because we're leaning to being long
cyclicals and short defensives.

318
00:32:00,000 --> 00:32:04,000
So if we were bearish on the world, we
wouldn't be looking at this trade at all,
we'd be looking at it the other way
around.

319
00:32:05,000 --> 00:32:18,000
So we've got the betas here where we've
given Chevron a beta of 1.32 and
AstraZeneca a beta of 1.12.

320
00:32:19,000 --> 00:32:22,000
These aren't the actual betas of Chevron
and AstraZeneca.

321
00:32:23,000 --> 00:32:29,000
The actual betas you can get online or in
the download section in the spreadsheets
that we've provided.

322
00:32:30,000 --> 00:32:36,000
We've given fictional betas for
illustrative purposes here, so don't worry
about it.

323
00:32:37,000 --> 00:32:39,000
You can get the real betas online or in
the download section.

324
00:32:40,000 --> 00:32:42,000
We're just using these betas for
illustrative purposes.

325
00:32:43,000 --> 00:32:50,000
So we've given Chevron a beta of 1.32 and
AstraZeneca a beta of 1.12.

326
00:32:52,000 --> 00:32:57,000
And the beta ratio between Chevron and
AstraZeneca is 1.1786.

327
00:32:58,000 --> 00:33:00,000
So what does that mean?

328
00:33:01,000 --> 00:33:15,000
It just means that for every one unit move
in the market, Chevron moves 1.1786 times
more than AstraZeneca.

329
00:33:16,000 --> 00:33:17,000
That's all it means.

330
00:33:18,000 --> 00:33:37,000
In terms of committing capital, what that
means is that for every dollar that you
put in to Chevron, going long Chevron, you
have to short 1.1786 times that $1.

331
00:33:38,000 --> 00:33:47,000
to hedge out the market risk in
AstraZeneca because AstraZeneca moves a
lot less than Chevron.

332
00:33:48,000 --> 00:33:52,000
So let's have a look at this in terms of
dollar commitment.

333
00:33:53,000 --> 00:34:14,000
So let's say, for example, you have a
restriction in your portfolio of $20,000
in one spread trade, meaning that your
self-imposed restriction for any one
spread trade is $20,000.

334
00:34:15,000 --> 00:34:28,000
So if you committed $10,000 long to
Chevron, you would have to short,
according to the beta ratio, $11,786 of
AstraZeneca.

335
00:34:29,000 --> 00:34:34,000
Well, if you did that, this would just
take you over your limit of $20,000.

336
00:34:35,000 --> 00:34:38,000
So your exposure would be 21.786.

337
00:34:39,000 --> 00:34:42,000
And that breaks your limit, so you can't
do that.

338
00:34:43,000 --> 00:35:07,000
So you would have to actually short,
sorry, you would have to actually go long
less than $10,000 in order to meet your
self-imposed exposure limit of $20,000.

339
00:35:08,000 --> 00:35:22,000
So to make sure that you're within your
limit, you would have to go long $9,180 of
Chevron and short $10,819 of AstraZeneca.

340
00:35:23,000 --> 00:35:26,000
Now, why are we doing this? Why are we
hedging out market risk?

341
00:35:27,000 --> 00:35:29,000
Well, let's think about this a bit more in
depth.

342
00:35:30,000 --> 00:35:35,000
So imagine if the market now fell 10%.

343
00:35:36,000 --> 00:35:39,000
Let's say we had this trade-on and the
market dropped 10%.

344
00:35:40,000 --> 00:35:48,000
So Chevron would drop 13.2% and
AstraZeneca would drop 11.2%.

345
00:35:49,000 --> 00:36:01,000
So in that situation, imagine if you were
just long $10,000 of Chevron and short
$10,000 of AstraZeneca.

346
00:36:04,000 --> 00:36:09,000
Well, all that would happen is you would
lose 2% on that trade.

347
00:36:10,000 --> 00:36:17,000
So you've basically got in that situation
an implied market risk.

348
00:36:17,000 --> 00:36:22,000
Because if the market drops 10%, you're
going to actually lose money on this
trade.

349
00:36:23,000 --> 00:36:32,000
Because Chevron moves for every one unit
that the market moves, Chevron moves more
than AstraZeneca.

350
00:36:33,000 --> 00:36:34,000
So what do you actually have to do?

351
00:36:36,000 --> 00:36:42,000
Well, what you have to do is actually
commit more capital on the side of
AstraZeneca.

352
00:36:43,000 --> 00:36:50,000
So now imagine if you use the beta ratio
and you hedge out this market risk.

353
00:36:51,000 --> 00:36:59,000
Well, if the market fell 10%, then you
would have less money committed on the
side of Chevron

354
00:37:00,000 --> 00:37:07,000
and more money committed on the side of
AstraZeneca versus the example of $10,000
long and $10,000 short.

355
00:37:08,000 --> 00:37:17,000
Which means if the market dropped by 10%,
even though Chevron would drop by 13.2%

356
00:37:18,000 --> 00:37:23,000
and AstraZeneca would drop by 11.2%, you
wouldn't lose any money on that trade.

357
00:37:24,000 --> 00:37:33,000
So on that move, you would actually come
out, roughly speaking, you would come out
not making or losing any money.

358
00:37:34,000 --> 00:37:38,000
Now the obvious question is, well, what
happens when the market goes up?

359
00:37:39,000 --> 00:37:42,000
Well, when the market goes up, it's the
same situation.

360
00:37:43,000 --> 00:37:53,000
If the market went up 10%, Chevron would
go up by 13.2% and AstraZeneca would go up
by 11.2%.

361
00:37:54,000 --> 00:37:58,000
And you wouldn't make or lose any money on
the trade.

362
00:37:59,000 --> 00:38:01,000
But that's missing the point.

363
00:38:02,000 --> 00:38:10,000
Because what you're actually doing here is
you're hedging out the market risk and
you're isolating sector and stock risk.

364
00:38:11,000 --> 00:38:18,000
So whether the market goes down 10% or
goes up 10%, it doesn't matter.

365
00:38:19,000 --> 00:38:24,000
Because the whole point of this trade is
that you're hedging out the market risk.

366
00:38:25,000 --> 00:38:37,000
So the statement that you're making here
when you put this trade on is that
regardless of what the market does,
Chevron is just going to outperform
AstraZeneca over time.

367
00:38:38,000 --> 00:38:59,000
So if you're bullish on the world, and in
this example we're assuming that you are,
where your world view is bullish and
you're leaning into buying cyclicals and
shorting defensives, the obvious question
is, well, if you beat a hedge and the
market goes up, then why would you do
that?

368
00:39:00,000 --> 00:39:01,000
Well, let's put it this way.

369
00:39:02,000 --> 00:39:07,000
If you wanted to have a market bet, then
you could just buy the market.

370
00:39:08,000 --> 00:39:10,000
So you just go and buy the S&P 500.

371
00:39:11,000 --> 00:39:22,000
But in this example, what you're actually
doing is hedging out the market risk so on
a day-to-day basis you don't make or lose
any money on this trade.

372
00:39:23,000 --> 00:39:41,000
But then when the news comes out at some
point that one of Chevron's competitors
has guided up their earnings and Chevron
goes up in sympathy, well, Chevron is just
going to go up and AstraZeneca is going to
do nothing.

373
00:39:42,000 --> 00:39:58,000
And imagine, for example, if the news came
out that Chevron was guiding up their
earnings, well, Chevron themselves would
just go up, let's say, 5%, 10%, and
AstraZeneca just wouldn't do anything.

374
00:39:59,000 --> 00:40:15,000
All you're doing here is hedging out the
market risk so that on a day-to-day basis,
on a normal day in the stock market, when
nothing is really happening and there's no
news out, you don't make or lose any
money.

375
00:40:16,000 --> 00:40:28,000
But also if there's an external shock,
because you're beta hedged, you should
come out of that situation not making or
losing any money.

376
00:40:29,000 --> 00:40:31,000
So that's why we beta hedge.

377
00:40:31,000 --> 00:40:37,000
That's why we hedge out market risk so we
can isolate the risk that we want to be
exposed to.

378
00:40:38,000 --> 00:40:50,000
As in this example, we want to be exposed
to cyclicality on the long side and we
want to be exposed to being short
non-cyclicality.

379
00:40:51,000 --> 00:40:59,000
So when the fundamental news flow comes
through that Chevron and that Chevron's
earnings are going higher,

380
00:41:00,000 --> 00:41:07,000
we're exposed to that but in the process
we're actually hedging out the overall
market risk.

381
00:41:08,000 --> 00:41:12,000
So you should be able to understand that
it's a fairly straightforward situation.

382
00:41:13,000 --> 00:41:23,000
Now what we can do is, for example, start
having a look at an example of where the
beta ratio is very, very high.

383
00:41:24,000 --> 00:41:28,000
So let's have a look, for example, if we
change the beta ratio to 3.

384
00:41:29,000 --> 00:41:36,000
Well, when we change the beta ratio to 3
or the beta ratio becomes very high,

385
00:41:37,000 --> 00:41:49,000
the gross exposure goes way out, way out
away from our self-imposed $20,000 spread
trade limit as our example.

386
00:41:50,000 --> 00:41:53,000
So what would we have to do?

387
00:41:56,000 --> 00:42:12,000
Well, we'd have to bring this right in to
$5,000 long in Chevron and short $15,000
of AstraZeneca.

388
00:42:13,000 --> 00:42:20,000
Now when you have very high beta ratios,
you really have to start asking yourself
the question.

389
00:42:21,000 --> 00:42:25,000
You know, on a very, very high beta ratio,
is this really an effective trade?

390
00:42:26,000 --> 00:42:35,000
Because for every $1 of capital I commit
on the long side, I have to commit $3 of
capital on the short side.

391
00:42:36,000 --> 00:42:49,000
That just means your short is extremely
capital hungry, meaning that this might
not actually be a trade where your capital
is best used.

392
00:42:50,000 --> 00:42:58,000
So what you're better off doing is
probably finding trades where the beta
ratio is actually lower.

393
00:42:59,000 --> 00:43:08,000
And I think you're probably better off
making sure that your beta ratio is below
2.

394
00:43:09,000 --> 00:43:20,000
You should really be questioning yourself
or questioning a trade in terms of capital
commitment that is, that has a beta ratio
above 2.

395
00:43:21,000 --> 00:43:35,000
Because it just means that the short or
the hedge is extremely capital hungry,
meaning that the stock on the short side
requires a lot of capital in order to
hedge out market risk.

396
00:43:36,000 --> 00:43:49,000
There should be more effective shorts or
more effective hedges out there that you
can commit capital to in order to hedge
out the market risk.

397
00:43:50,000 --> 00:43:54,000
So you should always be questioning a big
or large beta ratio.

398
00:43:55,000 --> 00:43:59,000
So that's the simple example.

399
00:44:00,000 --> 00:44:23,000
It's not too hard to understand what you
should be doing here in terms of your
processes is going through the idea
generation process, getting your trades
onto your watch list, as we've seen, and
managing your watch list properly and then
deciding when you want to put on your
trades.

400
00:44:24,000 --> 00:44:26,000
Whether you want to hedge out market risk.

401
00:44:27,000 --> 00:44:31,000
If you want to hedge out market risk, how
much of the market risk you want to hedge
out.

402
00:44:32,000 --> 00:44:35,000
If you want to hedge out all of the market
risk, that's totally fine.

403
00:44:36,000 --> 00:44:37,000
It's all about choices.

404
00:44:38,000 --> 00:44:51,000
And if you've got a net restriction in
terms of market exposure in your
portfolio, then you know that you're going
to have to fall inside that parameter in
order to have particular trades on.

405
00:44:52,000 --> 00:45:10,000
So just remember as well that having a
beta ratio above 2, you need to question
that really because it might not be an
effective hedge or an effective way to
hedge out the market risk.

406
00:45:11,000 --> 00:45:14,000
So that's the simple example.

407
00:45:15,000 --> 00:45:19,000
Let's now move on to the more complicated
example of theme betas.

408
00:45:20,000 --> 00:45:29,000
Okay, so the example we're going to look
at here is a slightly more complicated
example of how we use beta to hedge out
market risk.

409
00:45:30,000 --> 00:45:42,000
What we're looking at here is a theme or a
trend that you may have identified and you
want to actually play this theme in a
diversified way.

410
00:45:43,000 --> 00:45:54,000
So not just using one stock long and one
stock short, you want to actually
represent this theme or trend by having
two stocks long and two stocks short.

411
00:45:55,000 --> 00:46:01,000
And the theme we're looking at here is
discretionary versus essential retail
spend.

412
00:46:02,000 --> 00:46:08,000
So we're looking at high-end retailers,
long and short low-end retailers.

413
00:46:09,000 --> 00:46:16,000
And we're looking at how to hedge out
market risk in this trade.

414
00:46:17,000 --> 00:46:24,000
So we're looking at long Macy's and Dill
odds and short target and family dollar.

415
00:46:25,000 --> 00:46:29,000
So a couple of things here.

416
00:46:30,000 --> 00:46:39,000
If we're looking at going long
discretionary versus essential retail
spend or long high-end retailers versus
short low-end retailers.

417
00:46:40,000 --> 00:46:45,000
We're probably assuming here, well we are
assuming here, that our world view is
bullish.

418
00:46:46,000 --> 00:46:55,000
So we're assuming that at this stage we've
gone through all the leading indicators
and they're pointing to GDP growth.

419
00:46:56,000 --> 00:47:00,000
And we're expecting high GDP growth at
some point in the future.

420
00:47:01,000 --> 00:47:09,000
And now we're looking at the retail sector
and deciding how we express that view
across the retail sector.

421
00:47:10,000 --> 00:47:24,000
And if we're looking at long two stocks in
the retail sector and short two stocks in
the retail sector, essentially if we hedge
out market risk, we're also hedging out
sector risk.

422
00:47:25,000 --> 00:47:30,000
So what we're looking at here is an
intra-sector spread trade.

423
00:47:31,000 --> 00:47:40,000
So an intra-sector constituent spread
trade, the ones that we looked at earlier
in the presentation in previous videos.

424
00:47:41,000 --> 00:47:53,000
So let's have a look at this trade and try
to work out how we hedge out the market
risk when we've got two stocks long and
two stocks short.

425
00:47:54,000 --> 00:48:06,000
So let's assume in this portfolio we've
got a gross exposure of $100,000 and we
have a limit of $20,000 per theme.

426
00:48:07,000 --> 00:48:12,000
So a self-imposed limit where we can't go
over $20,000 for one theme.

427
00:48:13,000 --> 00:48:18,000
And what we've done here is we've assigned
beta values to the longs and the shorts.

428
00:48:19,000 --> 00:48:22,000
These beta values are not the real beta
values.

429
00:48:23,000 --> 00:48:28,000
These are fictional values that we've
assigned for illustrative purposes.

430
00:48:28,000 --> 00:48:36,000
So if you wanted to get the real ones,
just go online, get the real ones or get
them in the spreadsheets from the
downloads areas in the videos.

431
00:48:38,000 --> 00:48:45,000
So we're looking at four stocks here,
Macy's and Dillard's on the long side,
Target and Family Dollar on the short
side.

432
00:48:47,000 --> 00:48:52,000
We've assigned to Macy's a beta of 1.01
and Dillard's 1.27.

433
00:48:53,000 --> 00:48:57,000
Target 0.57 and Family Dollar 0.32.

434
00:48:58,000 --> 00:49:10,000
And the first thing we're going to do is
work out how we apportion this money to
the long side of the portfolio and the
short side of the portfolio.

435
00:49:11,000 --> 00:49:20,000
So what we do is we first work out the
gross beta, the total beta by adding up
all of these betas.

436
00:49:21,000 --> 00:49:31,000
So we get to 3.17 and we also add up the
beta of the long side and we get 2.28 and
the beta of the short side and we get
0.89.

437
00:49:32,000 --> 00:49:45,000
So intuitively, if we're going to
apportion this money, what we're seeing is
that the beta on the long side is a lot
higher than the beta on the short side.

438
00:49:46,000 --> 00:49:56,000
Which kind of makes sense because if we're
looking at cyclicality on the long side or
more cyclical stocks on the long side than
on the short side,

439
00:49:57,000 --> 00:50:04,000
you would expect higher betas on the long
side because the stocks, given that the
earnings are more sensitive to the
business cycle,

440
00:50:05,000 --> 00:50:15,000
the stocks are probably going to move more
given a one unit move in the market than
the stocks on the short side.

441
00:50:16,000 --> 00:50:19,000
So that's the first thing.

442
00:50:21,000 --> 00:50:31,000
When we're going to apportion this money,
if we're going to apportion this money,
it's very obvious that the majority of
this money has to be apportioned on the
short side

443
00:50:32,000 --> 00:50:37,000
and the minority of this money has to be
apportioned on the long side.

444
00:50:38,000 --> 00:50:41,000
But how much on either side? Well, we can
work it out accurately.

445
00:50:42,000 --> 00:50:47,000
So the next thing we do is we work out in
column F.

446
00:50:49,000 --> 00:50:55,000
We work out the beta contributions or
weightings, percentage weightings of each
beta.

447
00:50:57,000 --> 00:51:08,000
So Macy's, a beta of 1.01, divided by the
total beta of 3.17, gives 32%.

448
00:51:09,000 --> 00:51:15,000
Dillard's 40%, target 18%, family dollar
10%.

449
00:51:16,000 --> 00:51:28,000
We add up the beta or beta weightings for
the long side to get the beta weighting of
the long side to the total beta, which is
72%.

450
00:51:29,000 --> 00:51:30,000
And we do the same on the short side.

451
00:51:31,000 --> 00:51:35,000
We add up the weightings of the betas to
the total beta.

452
00:51:35,000 --> 00:51:45,000
And we get the short side contribution of
beta to the total beta.

453
00:51:46,000 --> 00:51:47,000
And this is 28%.

454
00:51:48,000 --> 00:51:56,000
So we've got 72% beta contribution from
the long side, and we've got 28% beta
contribution from the short side.

455
00:51:57,000 --> 00:52:02,000
Now, if we're apportioning this money, the
way we apportion it is actually the
inverse of these betas.

456
00:52:03,000 --> 00:52:11,000
So we flip them, and we apportion our
money 28% on the long side and 72% on the
short side.

457
00:52:12,000 --> 00:52:13,000
So that's the first thing we do.

458
00:52:14,000 --> 00:52:23,000
We've worked out, out of this $20,000, how
much of it gets apportioned to the long
side and how much gets apportioned to the
short side.

459
00:52:24,000 --> 00:52:30,000
Well, what gets apportioned to each side
is the inverse of the weightings of their
betas.

460
00:52:33,000 --> 00:52:42,000
And that's pretty obvious, because if
we're going to hedge out market risk, then
clearly, because the short side moves a
lot less than the long side,

461
00:52:43,000 --> 00:52:48,000
we have to apportion more of our money on
the short side than we do on the long
side.

462
00:52:49,000 --> 00:52:57,000
Now, the next thing we have to do, we have
to work out, for example, so if we stay on
the long side for a second,

463
00:52:58,000 --> 00:53:05,000
we have to work out, out of the 28% of the
$20,000 that we've apportioned on the long
side,

464
00:53:06,000 --> 00:53:15,000
what percentage of that 28% is apportioned
to Macy's, and what percentage is
apportioned to Dillard's?

465
00:53:16,000 --> 00:53:20,000
Well, let's use Macy's as an example, and
we'll show you how this is done.

466
00:53:21,000 --> 00:53:29,000
So the first thing we do is we work out
the contribution of the Macy's beta to the
long beta of the trade.

467
00:53:30,000 --> 00:53:35,000
So we divide 1.01 by 2.28, and we get 44%.

468
00:53:36,000 --> 00:53:46,000
With Dillard's, we divide 1.27 by 2.28,
and we get 56%.

469
00:53:47,000 --> 00:53:55,000
And then we work out, so let's have a look
at the formula bar here, H9 times G11.

470
00:53:56,000 --> 00:54:02,000
We work out 44% of the 28%, which gives us
12.44%.

471
00:54:03,000 --> 00:54:04,000
That's for Macy's.

472
00:54:05,000 --> 00:54:12,000
And for Dillard's, we work out 55%, for
56% of the 28%.

473
00:54:13,000 --> 00:54:22,000
And then, when we're apportioning our
money on the long side, we then have to
flip and do the inverse again

474
00:54:23,000 --> 00:54:30,000
the percentage that we have to apportion
in Macy's and Dillard's.

475
00:54:30,000 --> 00:54:44,000
So, in Macy's, we have to apportion 15.64%
of our money, and in Dillard's, we have to
apportion 12.44% of our money.

476
00:54:45,000 --> 00:55:00,000
And this 15.64% and this 12.44% is the
total amount of the $20,000 that's
apportioned to the whole trade.

477
00:55:01,000 --> 00:55:10,000
So, if we look at the short side, we'll
see that, if we go through the same
exercise, because Family Dollar has the
lowest beta,

478
00:55:11,000 --> 00:55:20,000
when we repeat the same process on the
short side, Family Dollar, 46% of our
money has to be exposed to Family Dollar,

479
00:55:21,000 --> 00:55:24,000
26% has to be exposed to Target.

480
00:55:27,000 --> 00:55:38,000
And when we add up on the long side, the
percentages in the way that our money is
apportioned, we get the amount of money
that is apportioned to the long side.

481
00:55:39,000 --> 00:55:59,000
And if we add up the percentages in the
way that our money is apportioned on the
short side, we get the percentage, 72%, we
get the percentage of our money that's
apportioned to the short side, making
100%.

482
00:56:00,000 --> 00:56:12,000
So, we get the percentage of our money
that's apportioned to each position.

483
00:56:14,000 --> 00:56:18,000
Then, we work out this in dollar terms.

484
00:56:18,000 --> 00:56:27,000
So, we're working out, out of the 20,000,
so 15.64% of the 20,000, so just over
$3,000.

485
00:56:28,000 --> 00:56:31,000
In Dillard's, $2,487.

486
00:56:32,000 --> 00:56:37,000
Total dollars exposed on the long side,
$5,615.

487
00:56:39,000 --> 00:56:48,000
On the short side, in Target, we're
apportioning $5,172.

488
00:56:50,000 --> 00:56:59,000
And in Family Dollar, we're apportioning,
out of the $20,000, $9,213.

489
00:57:00,000 --> 00:57:04,000
So, in total, $14,385.

490
00:57:05,000 --> 00:57:12,000
So, that works out to be 72% and 28%.

491
00:57:15,000 --> 00:57:21,000
We take the prices of each stock, and this
tells us how many shares that we have to
buy and sell.

492
00:57:22,000 --> 00:57:31,000
And then, we're also working out here the
percentage size of these positions in our
portfolio.

493
00:57:32,000 --> 00:57:34,000
So, this is just K9.

494
00:57:35,000 --> 00:57:41,000
So, this is $3,128 divided by the
$100,000.

495
00:57:42,000 --> 00:57:45,000
And we do this for each position, and the
long side, and the short side.

496
00:57:46,000 --> 00:57:51,000
So, each position on the long side, and
the short side, and the total long side,
and the short side.

497
00:57:54,000 --> 00:58:05,000
So, that's how you work out, from your
beta values, how you hedge out the market
risk in a theme that has two longs and two
shorts.

498
00:58:06,000 --> 00:58:13,000
Now, you can get more complicated if you
want, where you have three longs and three
shorts, four longs and four shorts.

499
00:58:14,000 --> 00:58:16,000
But, I wouldn't advise doing that to begin
with.

500
00:58:17,000 --> 00:58:29,000
So, what you should probably be doing when
you start out is to try and make money
just doing spread trades, one stock long,
one stock short.

501
00:58:30,000 --> 00:58:36,000
And then, when you're making money,
graduate to doing two stocks long and two
stocks short.

502
00:58:37,000 --> 00:58:42,000
And if you can make money doing this, then
you can get more complicated later on.

503
00:58:43,000 --> 00:58:47,000
You can get a little bit smarter and a bit
more cleverer once you're making money.

504
00:58:48,000 --> 00:58:51,000
So, you can see this is a little bit
complicated.

505
00:58:52,000 --> 00:58:54,000
You've got this spreadsheet in the
downloads area.

506
00:58:55,000 --> 00:59:01,000
So, just download it and have a go at
reverse engineering the sheet yourself.

507
00:59:02,000 --> 00:59:04,000
It's not that difficult to get used to.

508
00:59:05,000 --> 00:59:24,000
So, these trades are quite typical trades
where if you have an idea, but you don't
want to have your exposure in just long
one stock and short one stock, you can
actually represent the same idea by
diversifying a little bit into two longs
and two shorts.

509
00:59:25,000 --> 00:59:35,000
So, that concludes hedging out market risk
using beta across two longs and two
shorts.

510
00:59:36,000 --> 00:59:40,000
Now, we're going to move on to the next
video and I'll see you in the next video.

511
00:59:42,000 --> 00:59:43,000
Okay, welcome back.

512
00:59:43,000 --> 01:00:07,000
So, that was an introduction to beta and
what we looked at there was beta across
two stocks and beta across a theme, as in
a theme that you've identified that you
believe that will happen as an overall
idea or trend within a certain industry.

513
01:00:07,000 --> 01:00:18,000
And how you can represent that theme and
hedge out the market risk at the same time
is by having multiple longs and multiple
shorts representing the theme.

514
01:00:19,000 --> 01:00:21,000
And you hedge out market risk in the
process.

515
01:00:22,000 --> 01:00:27,000
And if you mechanically want to do that,
then just refer to that spreadsheet and go
through the steps.

516
01:00:28,000 --> 01:00:32,000
With two single stocks, it's very
straightforward.

517
01:00:33,000 --> 01:00:41,000
We just take the beta and the beta ratio
and this gives us our positioning and
sizes that we must put on on either side
of the trade.

518
01:00:42,000 --> 01:00:50,000
Now, what we're going to do is do
something that you may not think occurs
immediately.

519
01:00:51,000 --> 01:00:57,000
What we're going to do is have a look at
how we hedge out currency risk on a trade
across two geographies.

520
01:00:58,000 --> 01:01:08,000
So, for example, if you take a position in
the United Kingdom and take a position in
the United States, you're actually going
to have an implied currency exposure.

521
01:01:09,000 --> 01:01:12,000
And what we're going to look at is how we
hedge this exposure out.

522
01:01:13,000 --> 01:01:20,000
Then we'll do a full summary of the risk
management parameters that we've looked at
for the portfolio.

523
01:01:21,000 --> 01:01:27,000
But first, let's go over to the screen and
I'll show you how you effectively hedge
out currency risk.

524
01:01:32,000 --> 01:01:35,000
OK, so welcome back to the computer
screen.

525
01:01:35,000 --> 01:01:39,000
What we're going to have a look at here is
currency hedging.

526
01:01:40,000 --> 01:01:47,000
And what you'll typically find when you do
cross country spread trades.

527
01:01:48,000 --> 01:01:56,000
So a spread trade where you have a long
and a short in two different geographical
regions with two different currencies.

528
01:01:57,000 --> 01:02:03,000
What you'll find is that when you do these
trades, you're going to end up with an
implied currency risk.

529
01:02:04,000 --> 01:02:10,000
So what we're going to look at first is
just the idea of a currency as a spread
trade.

530
01:02:11,000 --> 01:02:16,000
We're just going to pop back into the
watch list example, which you have in the
downloads area.

531
01:02:17,000 --> 01:02:24,000
And we're just going to go into the
presentation and have a look at the one
particular slide which explains currency
risk.

532
01:02:25,000 --> 01:02:28,000
So let's open up the watch list.

533
01:02:30,000 --> 01:02:36,000
And this will be familiar to you where we
have all of our trade ideas on the example
watch list.

534
01:02:37,000 --> 01:02:47,000
And if you scroll down, you'll see that we
have a section for Forex and we also have
a section for commodities, energy, metals
and food.

535
01:02:48,000 --> 01:03:02,000
First things first, we just need to get
over this concept that a Forex trade is
actually just a spread trade by default.

536
01:03:03,000 --> 01:03:06,000
So a currency trade is a spread trade by
default.

537
01:03:07,000 --> 01:03:20,000
If you think about it, a currency trade is
a spread trade because you can't actually
buy a currency without selling another
currency first.

538
01:03:21,000 --> 01:03:29,000
So when you're putting your currency
trades into your watch list, there is no
long and there is no short price.

539
01:03:30,000 --> 01:03:31,000
There is just one entry price.

540
01:03:32,000 --> 01:03:41,000
So if we have a look at EURUSD for
example, well, you can't buy dollars
without selling euros first in this
currency pair.

541
01:03:42,000 --> 01:03:43,000
So the price is just the price.

542
01:03:43,000 --> 01:04:06,000
And when you're putting and updating your
spreadsheet with your currency pairs, your
reference prices, when you're looking at
them, you put them on your watch list just
in the spread area here and
correspondingly in the spread column here.

543
01:04:07,000 --> 01:04:21,000
And then when you're updating the currency
pair for the reference prices in the
current situation, you put them in the
current price here in the current spread.

544
01:04:22,000 --> 01:04:34,000
So let's say, for example, EURUSD was on
the watch list seven weeks ago.

545
01:04:35,000 --> 01:04:40,000
Well, seven weeks ago, it was around 128,
129.

546
01:04:41,000 --> 01:04:47,000
And we put it again in here as the
reference price.

547
01:04:47,000 --> 01:04:48,000
And then we have it here.

548
01:04:49,000 --> 01:04:54,000
The current situation, which is EURUSD is
trading at 131.

549
01:04:55,000 --> 01:04:57,000
So you just update it in that way.

550
01:04:58,000 --> 01:05:00,000
There is no long and there is no short
entry.

551
01:05:01,000 --> 01:05:06,000
That's just the price of buying dollars
and selling euros to do so.

552
01:05:07,000 --> 01:05:08,000
So that's the first thing.

553
01:05:09,000 --> 01:05:10,000
Now we're going to look at currency
hedging.

554
01:05:10,000 --> 01:05:23,000
So what actually happens when you have a
long position in a stock or an asset in
one currency and a short position in a
stock or an asset in another currency.

555
01:05:24,000 --> 01:05:26,000
So they're denominated in different
currencies.

556
01:05:27,000 --> 01:05:44,000
Well, this comes into our portfolio
construction parameters area where you're
probably going to be setting this
parameter in your portfolio by making the
statement, well, if I do these trades, I
should just hedge out my currency risk to
begin with.

557
01:05:45,000 --> 01:05:51,000
And it's fairly straightforward to
understand and very easy to do on pretty
much all brokerage platforms.

558
01:05:52,000 --> 01:06:02,000
So instead of having a situation where you
go longer stock and shorter stock in the
same currency, we're now looking at doing
it in two different currencies.

559
01:06:03,000 --> 01:06:07,000
And what that implies is that you have a
currency position.

560
01:06:08,000 --> 01:06:10,000
So let's go through this example.

561
01:06:11,000 --> 01:06:26,000
Let's say, for example, we wanted to buy
£20,000 of Standard Chartered, which is an
Asian bank with a listing in Asia, UK and
in the United States.

562
01:06:27,000 --> 01:06:30,000
But we wanted to do this in pounds in
London.

563
01:06:31,000 --> 01:06:45,000
So we're looking at buying £20,000 of
Standard Chartered and going short the
equivalent amount, £20,000 of Santander,
the Spanish bank.

564
01:06:46,000 --> 01:06:58,000
So Standards is actually denominated in
sterling and Santander denominated in US
dollars if we're trading the New York
Stock Exchange listing.

565
01:06:59,000 --> 01:07:10,000
Now, let's say the reference prices in the
market are £17.22 for Standards in London.

566
01:07:11,000 --> 01:07:17,000
And for Santander in New York, the
reference price is $7.56.

567
01:07:18,000 --> 01:07:23,000
And let's just assume that the sterling US
dollar cable rate is one and a half.

568
01:07:24,000 --> 01:07:30,000
So £20,000 worth of Santander is
equivalent to $30,000.

569
01:07:31,000 --> 01:07:43,000
Now, what happens is when we do a spread
trade, the stock that we purchase, we have
to pay for that purchase with the currency
that we're purchasing it in.

570
01:07:44,000 --> 01:07:47,000
And when we short a stock, we actually
receive that currency.

571
01:07:48,000 --> 01:07:52,000
Because when you're selling, you receive
the money for selling the stock.

572
01:07:53,000 --> 01:07:56,000
So what you end up with is an implied
currency risk.

573
01:07:57,000 --> 01:08:04,000
So in this example, if we buy Standard
Chartered, we pay pounds for the stock.

574
01:08:05,000 --> 01:08:08,000
And if we short Santander, we receive the
US dollars.

575
01:08:09,000 --> 01:08:12,000
Therefore, we end up receiving 30,000 US
dollars.

576
01:08:14,000 --> 01:08:16,000
And we're short £20,000.

577
01:08:17,000 --> 01:08:22,000
So what you have to do to hedge out the
currency risk here is you actually have to
do three trades.

578
01:08:23,000 --> 01:08:26,000
You have to buy Standard Chartered.

579
01:08:27,000 --> 01:08:29,000
You have to short Santander.

580
01:08:30,000 --> 01:08:36,000
And then you have to buy £20,000 versus US
dollar to hedge out the currency risk.

581
01:08:37,000 --> 01:08:39,000
So you do three trades in total.

582
01:08:40,000 --> 01:08:41,000
And you do them all at the same time.

583
01:08:42,000 --> 01:08:44,000
Now, why is this a risk?

584
01:08:45,000 --> 01:08:50,000
Well, simply because if, for example, the
spread moves 10% in our favour,

585
01:08:51,000 --> 01:09:00,000
but the currency moves 10% against us
during the lifetime that we have this
spread on,

586
01:09:01,000 --> 01:09:04,000
then when we unwind the spread at the same
time,

587
01:09:05,000 --> 01:09:09,000
so when we actually come to trade out of
the spread,

588
01:09:10,000 --> 01:09:13,000
so selling Standard Chartered and buying
back Santander,

589
01:09:14,000 --> 01:09:16,000
if the currency has moved against us,

590
01:09:17,000 --> 01:09:19,000
that just means we won't actually make any
money.

591
01:09:20,000 --> 01:09:24,000
So what you have to do is actually just
hedge out this currency risk right at the
beginning.

592
01:09:25,000 --> 01:09:27,000
So if the spread does move in your favour,

593
01:09:28,000 --> 01:09:34,000
the currency move doesn't cancel out the
move in the spread.

594
01:09:35,000 --> 01:09:37,000
So that's pretty straightforward.

595
01:09:38,000 --> 01:09:43,000
If you're doing this, just make sure that
you're aware of the currency risk

596
01:09:44,000 --> 01:09:49,000
and make sure that if you don't want to
have that risk that you just hedge it out.

597
01:09:50,000 --> 01:09:52,000
And you can do that very, very easily.

598
01:09:52,000 --> 01:09:54,000
You just work out what your currency
exposure is.

599
01:09:55,000 --> 01:10:03,000
So what you're paying for, what currency
you're paying in and what currency you're
receiving,

600
01:10:04,000 --> 01:10:09,000
and then do the opposite trade and just
hedge it out and do all three of the
trades at once.

601
01:10:10,000 --> 01:10:11,000
So you don't have the currency risk.

602
01:10:11,000 --> 01:10:16,000
And then when you unwind, you just unwind
all three of the positions at the same
time.

603
01:10:17,000 --> 01:10:19,000
So what are you going to see in your
brokerage account?

604
01:10:19,000 --> 01:10:21,000
You're just going to see three positions.

605
01:10:22,000 --> 01:10:29,000
You're going to see, in this example,
you're going to see long Standard
Chartered,

606
01:10:30,000 --> 01:10:31,000
15,000 pounds worth.

607
01:10:32,000 --> 01:10:34,000
You're going to see short Santander.

608
01:10:36,000 --> 01:10:38,000
And you're going to see short $30,000
worth.

609
01:10:39,000 --> 01:10:43,000
And then you're going to see a long
sterling US dollar position,

610
01:10:44,000 --> 01:10:47,000
equivalent to 20,000 pounds or 30,000 US
dollars.

611
01:10:48,000 --> 01:10:49,000
And that's basically it.

612
01:10:50,000 --> 01:10:52,000
And then you just unwind that position, or
all three positions,

613
01:10:53,000 --> 01:10:55,000
when you want to trade out of the spread.

614
01:10:55,000 --> 01:10:58,000
So whether you're stopped out or you're
taking profits,

615
01:10:59,000 --> 01:11:02,000
whenever you unwind, you just unwind all
three of them.

616
01:11:03,000 --> 01:11:04,000
So very straightforward to understand.

617
01:11:05,000 --> 01:11:06,000
Try to get your head around it.

618
01:11:07,000 --> 01:11:11,000
It's just one of those construction
parameters that professional traders
understand

619
01:11:12,000 --> 01:11:16,000
and make sure that they hedge out whenever
they can or if necessary.

620
01:11:17,000 --> 01:11:18,000
So just understand it.

621
01:11:19,000 --> 01:11:21,000
And when you get a little bit more
advanced,

622
01:11:22,000 --> 01:11:24,000
as in when you begin spread trading,

623
01:11:25,000 --> 01:11:28,000
you should probably just start by going
long and short stocks

624
01:11:28,000 --> 01:11:30,000
that are denominated in the same currency.

625
01:11:31,000 --> 01:11:34,000
But, you know, when you're making money
and you're getting a bit more advanced,

626
01:11:35,000 --> 01:11:40,000
then this is something you'll probably
come across because you'll start wanting
to do spread trades

627
01:11:41,000 --> 01:11:43,000
across geographies and across different
currencies.

628
01:11:44,000 --> 01:11:47,000
And all you have to do is just understand
your currency risk, hedge it out,

629
01:11:48,000 --> 01:11:51,000
and then when you unwind, you unwind all
positions at the same time.

